    71	**1** **INTRODUCTION**
    72	
    73	
    74	Blockchains and cryptocurrencies have become an integral part
    75	of our economy. As of the writing of this paper, the global market
    76	cap for cryptocurrencies reaches $1.19 Trillion USD, with the top
    77	two blockchains being Bitcoin and Ethereum [ 6 ]. An important
    78	kind of blockchain-based applications are smart contracts, which
    79	can encompass a wide range of services, from banks to gaming
    80	platforms and marketplaces. Smart contracts follow the DeFi, or
    81	_decentralized finance_ principle. Unlike centralized systems, such as
    82	federal banks, smart contracts operate in a decentralized manner
    83	without a single controlling authority, rendering many novel financial applications. Similar to traditional software, smart contracts are
    84	developed by programmers and inevitably have bugs. The lucrative
    85	value of exploiting these bugs has made smart contracts one of the
    86	most popular targets of many malicious actors. As of Q2 of 2023,
    87	$300 million USD were exploited from 212 security incidents [ 10 ],
    88	suggesting that each exploit costed an average of $1.5 million USD.
    89	Therefore, there is a pressing need to develop techniques to
    90	find smart contract bugs. Existing techniques can be roughly classified into four categories: static analysis, fuzzers, symbolic execution, and verification. Static analysis tools [ 18, 23, 26, 27, 35,
    91	39, 60, 65, 68, 71, 79 ] analyze source code without actually running the code. They usually transform smart contracts to various intermediate representations and then search for certain bug
    92	patterns. Fuzzers [ 24, 28, 29, 34, 36, 45, 54, 67, 69, 70, 74, 75 ] run
    93	contracts against a large number of inputs and transactions sequences. Symbolic execution [ 1, 17, 19, 43, 51, 52, 55, 61, 67, 73 ]
    94	analyzes all possible program paths of a smart contract by performing symbolic computation instead of concrete execution. Verification tools [ 30, 32, 62, 63, 72 ] leverage formal methods to check
    95	smart contracts against formal specifications. These approaches
    96	have demonstrated great effectiveness in identifying a broad range
    97	of issues. Some bugs such as _reentrancy_ and integer _overflow_ and
    98	_underflow_ can hardly survive these tools. However, most automatic
    99	techniques rely on application agnostic oracles, meaning that bugs
   100	need to be clearly defined without considering application specific
   101	semantics. Such oracles may be difficult to acquire for certain kinds
   102	of bugs. Verification tools are capable of detecting a wide spectrum
   103	of bugs including those that are application specific. However, they
   104	need the developers to provide application specifications, which
   105	may entail substantial manual efforts. As a result, there are still a
   106	large number of bugs that are beyond existing tools, evidenced by
   107	the growing number of exploits.
   108	According to a recent study by Zhang et al. [ 78 ] on over 500
   109	exploitable bugs (bugs that can lead to direct fund loss) from 119
   110	real-world smart contract projects, 80% of exploitable bugs are _ma-_
   111	_chine unauditable bugs_ (MUBs), meaning that they fall outside of
   112	the scope of existing automatic tools. Among them, _accounting_
   113	_bugs_, which are incorrect implementations of underlying contract
   114	
   115	
   116	ICSE ’24, April 14–20, 2024, Lisbon, Portugal Brian Zhang
   117	
   118	
   119	
   120	business models, are the most common type of MUBs in projects
   121	before deployment and also the second hardest to find in manual
   122	auditing, due to the need of understanding the most complex parts
   123	of contracts, namely, the business logics. On the other hand, their
   124	impact can be devastating. An example would be the Uranium Finance Exploit [ 14 ]. Due to two extra zeros in an interest calculation,
   125	the contract was exploited for $57 million USD. The bug survived
   126	multiple rounds of manual auditing (by experts).
   127	In this paper, we develop a type-checking tool for accounting
   128	bugs in smart contracts. Our insight is that _although accounting bugs_
   129	_reside in complex business logic and seemingly lack an application-_
   130	_agnostic oracle, many manifest themselves as abstract type violations_ .
   131	_Abstract type inference_ is a technique that can be traced back to the
   132	70’s in the last century [ 40 ]. It aims to abstract higher level semantic
   133	information such as physical units (e.g., seconds and meters) than
   134	those denoted by primitive types in programming languages such as
   135	integers and strings. As such, type systems can be enhanced to check
   136	a much richer set of properties [ 16, 31, 33, 40 – 42, 50, 56, 59, 61 ],
   137	such as physical unit consistency in robotic systems. We further
   138	observe that _although smart contracts have sophisticated business_
   139	_models, their basic operations are still analogous to those in a simple_
   140	_bank system_, such as deposit, withdraw, exchange, and loan. We
   141	hence devise an abstract type system based on these operations
   142	that can infer and check abstract types. In particular, we model and
   143	infer three facets of each variable, which are: _token unit_ indicating
   144	the kind of currency denoted by the variable (analogous to USD
   145	in real life), _scaling factor_ that denotes how much the variable
   146	has been scaled in order to simulate floating point computation
   147	that is not supported in smart contract programming languages,
   148	and _financial meaning_, e.g., if the variable denotes an interest or a
   149	debt. With the rich types, we can check a large set of properties
   150	that shall be uniformly true for various business models, using
   151	type rules. For instance, values of different token units cannot be
   152	added or subtracted together, similar to how lengths of meters and
   153	inches cannot be added together; amounts scaled by different factors
   154	should not be compared; interest should not be subtracted from
   155	debt but rather adds to it. More details can be found in section 3. To
   156	
   157	use our tool, the user annotates a few global variables and function
   158	parameters whose abstract types cannot be inferred from code
   159	(likely because they are implicit in pre-conditions and not reflected
   160	in implementation). The annotations are limited (see section 4)
   161	and usually clear from project description and even variable names.
   162	Then, our technique automatically infers the abstract types for other
